<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toribio Montes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1749">1749</a>–<a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toribio Montes was a Spanish general sent by the viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> to put down the autonomous government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. His forces took Quito in November 1812 and destroyed the last patriot army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibarra">Ibarra</a> in December. As president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-audiencia-of-quito">Real Audiencia of Quito</a> until 1817 he followed a relatively conciliatory policy towards the defeated patriots.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toribio-montes.jpg" alt="Toribio Montes" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toribio_Montes_y_P%C3%A9rez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Toribio Montes was baptised on 7 May 1749 in the Cantabrian village of San Mamés, into a military family of rural hidalgos; his father, Pedro Montes-Caloca, was a councillor and trustee for the Polaciones valley. He joined the Prince's Infantry Regiment as a captain in 1766, served more than four years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> and was wounded in the Invasion of Algiers of 1775. With the America Regiment he took part in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> in 1779 and the reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> he fought at Peyrestortes and Truillas, and as a brigadier commanding the Murcia Regiment he was wounded in 1794 and lost the use of three fingers. He was later governor of the castle of Montjuic, became a field marshal in 1802 after the War of the Oranges, and from 1804 governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> as captain general for almost five years.</p><p>Appointed in November 1811 to preside over the Royal Audience of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and command the Spanish army there, he and Brigadier Juan de Sámano defeated the patriots of the independent State of Quito and restored the Crown's authority there; he was promoted lieutenant general in 1812. He then turned north against the patriots of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-narino">Antonio Nariño</a>'s campaign was defeated before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasto">Pasto</a> in 1814 and Nariño captured, Montes, who had ordered the execution of other captured patriot officers, commuted his sentence and sent him to prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. A royalist offensive took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a> on 29 December 1814 but was beaten at the Battle of the Palo River on 5 July 1815, and Montes recalled Sámano, who defeated the insurgents at Cuchilla del Tambo in 1816.</p><p>Montes left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> on 26 July 1817 and reached Cádiz on 29 April 1819. He was made a member of the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> and San Fernando and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">Military Junta</a> of the Indies. After sixty-three years in the army he asked to retire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>; when absolutism was restored after the invasion of the Hundred Thousand Sons of Saint Louis, he was given command of the province until he was replaced on 31 December 1827.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toribio Montes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Forces sent by the viceroy of Peru under <b>Toribio Montes</b> took Quito in November 1812 and defeated the last patriots at Ibarra in December.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-quito">State of Quito</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,-1.13&amp;year=1801" aria-label="Murcia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Toribio Montes"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4924 4319h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4924 4319h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,-1.13&amp;year=1801">What was here in 1801? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
